{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 2, :comments ["<b>Who aroused…</b> The ancients said that this is an allusion to Abraham, who overpowered the kings and shattered the idols, and the proof is that afterward he said, \"the seed of Abraham my friend\"; this too is sound. In my opinion it is an allusion to Cyrus, for the entire pericope is connected, and so it is written, \"one who calls from the east, a bird of prey\" (Isaiah 46:11), and, \"I have aroused one from the north and he has come, from the east one who calls on my name\" (Isaiah 41:25), and the entire pericope explicitly concerns Cyrus." "<b>From the east.</b> For Elam is northeast of Babylonia." "<b>Righteousness calls him to his foot.</b> He encounters, or righteousness calls him wherever he goes." "<b>And kings shall come down.</b> He causes others to rule over kings, for this word is of the hif'il binyan, and the meaning is like \"and he shall cause to come down from Jacob\" (Numbers 24:19)." "<b>His sword is given as dust.</b> The sword of every king. Others say that he shall have many swords and his arrows shall fly like driven chaff."], :book :isaiah, :voice :ibn-ezra, :lang :en, :chapter 41, :notes {0 "The second citation 'מ״א כ״ה' is ambiguous; it likely refers to Isaiah 41:25 (chapter 41, verse 25), not 1 Kings, since the quoted phrase matches Isaiah 41:25."}, :source-witnesses [["Ibn Ezra on Isaiah, by M. Friedlander; Society of Hebrew Literature, London 1877" "https://www.nli.org.il/he/books/NNL_ALEPH001338443"]], :timestamp "2026-07-09T18:57:20.451293015Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}